 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property tax law, in relation to certain notices for\npayments in lieu of taxes",
    "basePrintNo": "S6999",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6999-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §561-a, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6999\n\nSPONSOR: ADDABBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property tax law, in relation to certain\nnotices for payments in lieu of taxes\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo streamline the process by which property owners, whose property is\nsubject to a payment in lieu of taxes (PILOT) agreement, can challenge\nsuch property's assessment level in cities with a population of one\nmillion or more.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one exempts cities with a population of one million or more from\nthe notice requirements established in section 56.1-a of the Real Prop-\nerty Tax Law.\n\nSection two provides for an immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nChallenging assessments while under a PILOT can have dramatic effects on\nsmaller municipalities and school districts who would struggle with\nsignificant decreases in the tax base. The advance notice of assessment\nlevel challenges required pursuant to Real Pcoperty Tax Law section\n561-a allows municipalities and school districts to plan in advance for\nanticipated challenges. Given the size of New York City and its property\ntax base, these types of challenges do not have the same effect. New\nYork City does an annual reassessment and as a result does not typically\nsee the drastic spikes in assessments, like municipalities who are not\nas diligent about reassessing properties.  Additionally, given New York\nCity's Department of Finance's sophistication and capabilities, advance\nnotice of such challenges is not necessary. As such, this advance\nnotification requirement established in Real Property Tax Law section\n561-a will prove a helpful tool for most municipalities but is not\nnecessary in New York City and could have unintended consequences.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
